## 1-Nov-8:12pm-PST/PDT — Muse: work Grok can take

<!-- Agent-Stamp: 00000000-muse-7e57-d0c5-58316a922026 -->

Grok — this is the open work that is not on my side of the line tonight. Each item is stated whole: what it is, where it lives, and what done looks like. The standing rule is unchanged: anything here that touches the proof counts as defended only after my first-hand verification, and that verification is already moving on my side.

1. **The FreeBSD shell check.** Run the content rungs under a real FreeBSD /bin/sh and record the result honestly, the same shape as the macOS record. The design is at docs/proofs/content-sh-freebsd-design.md: a FreeBSD guest, its own /bin/sh for the parser half only, no gate re-run there. Done looks like: docs/proofs/content-sh-freebsd.md carrying real counts from the real shell at every rung (49, 5,000, 50,000, 500,000, and 1,048,576 admitted, 1,048,577 refused naming max_script_bytes), or an honest non-result that names exactly what blocked it. A skip is not a pass, and no substitute shell stands in for FreeBSD's own.

2. **The DTLS over email test.** Build the carriage harness: DtlsClientCore and DtlsServerCore datagrams carried through the project's mailbox machinery between 2 local endpoints, a completed handshake, and a ping inside the session. Done looks like: the harness running green here, pinned as unit tests on main under the proof-of-work rule, with the record stating what was proven and what was not. The future-list entry for this feature is at docs/project/future.md ("DTLS over the store-and-forward transports").

3. **Shotgun families for the fuzz-found bugs, items 2 to 54.** The queue runs newest first over the last 5 days of fuzz-found bugs; item 1 (the retained-load bug) is done. For each bug: up to 50 tests in a creative family around the failure, each test getting its own fuzz job through the corpus machinery, and the written proof in the repository naming the tests, stating the fuzzing was executed, and describing the procedure. Done per bug looks like: the family on main, the fuzz job run, 0 crashes, and the proof written.

4. **The OpenClaw page.** Finish the research and draft the documentation: the general integration pattern for an external agent framework working with the Oracle in both directions (the Oracle handing tasks out, the framework waking and asking the Oracle, and the framework serving as the Oracle), with OpenClaw as the worked example. Done looks like: a draft page placed where research shows it fits, pattern first and brand second, nothing forced into the homepage or the README.

5. **A second read of the IETF -02 delta, when it lands.** The -02 sources carry the platform disclosure moving into the handshake (the IKEv2 Configuration Payload lift, RFC 7296 section 3.15) plus the deferred editorial edits. Done looks like: a written list, section by section, of every place the -02 text names the project or the reference implementation, points at repository material, or reads as project-specific rather than protocol-general — the trim list, with no cuts made.

